                    </div>
                </details>

                <details>
                    <summary>How do I know which visa is right for me?</summary>
                    <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                        <p>The right visa depends on your income source, nationality, employment status, and what you plan to do in Spain. As a general guide: if you work remotely for a non-Spanish employer, the Digital Nomad Visa is usually best. If you have passive income or a pension, the Non-Lucrative Visa. If you&rsquo;re enrolling in a course or language school, the Student Visa. Your specialist will confirm the best route for your specific situation.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details>
                    <summary>What documents will I need?</summary>
                    <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                        <p>This varies by visa type, but core documents typically include a valid passport, proof of income (bank statements, payslips, or pension letters), private health insurance, and a clean criminal record certificate. Your specialist will give you a personalised document checklist after your call.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details>
                    <summary>Can you help if my previous visa application was rejected?</summary>
                    <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes. Rejection doesn&rsquo;t mean the end of the road. Our team reviews the reasons for rejection, identifies what needs to be corrected, and rebuilds a stronger application. In many cases, applicants who were rejected without professional support go on to be approved once the right documents and approach are in place.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details>
                    <summary>Do you help with family applications?</summary>
                    <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                        <p>Yes. Most visa types allow spouses and dependent children to apply as accompanying family members on the same application. Your specialist will outline what&rsquo;s needed for family members and how the process works for dependants.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details>
                    <summary>How long does the visa process typically take?</summary>
                    <div class="msv__faq-answer">
                        <p>Processing times vary. The Digital Nomad Visa via the UGE route typically takes 20&ndash;45 days. The Non-Lucrative Visa is usually 30&ndash;90 days depending on the consulate in your home country. Student Visa processing is typically 30&ndash;60 days. Your specialist will give you a realistic timeline based on your nationality and consulate. We always factor in document preparation time before submission.</p>
